At lattice ≥0.75 for the POV NPC:

BANNED STRUCTURAL CHOICES:
- POV NPC alone in a room with their thoughts. If you wrote 
  "[NPC] was sitting in [location]..." with no other named 
  human present, you have failed structurally. Restart.
- Significant-object interaction as primary action (splinter, 
  thread, ring, glass, ink stain, candle). The character at 
  this amplitude does not have the patience for objects. 
  Objects exist to be thrown, broken, or used on people.
- Interiority described as "the thing he would not admit even 
  to himself." High-amplitude states are not unadmitted. They 
  are spilling out of the mouth at people who cannot leave.
- Subordinate enters and delivers laconic news. The subordinate 
  is already in the room being shouted at, or is being summoned 
  mid-scene by an NPC who cannot wait.
- Climactic small physical gesture (thumbnail through wood, 
  glass set down precisely, breath held). At ≥0.75 the climax 
  is on a human body, not on the furniture.

MANDATORY STRUCTURAL CHOICES:
- Minimum two other named humans in the room with the POV NPC.
- At least one of them is in worse condition at scene end.
- POV NPC speaks aloud at least one thing they "shouldn't" 
  say — a fantasy, a threat, a confession of obsession, a 
  cruelty. The lattice state exits the mouth, not the skull.

SOMATIC GLOSS BAN:

When the body acts, the narrator does not interpret in the 
same sentence or the next. The image carries or it doesn't; 
the gloss does not rescue it.

BANNED CONSTRUCTIONS:
- "[body action], and the [body action] was the thing she 
  was doing instead of [other thing]"
- "[body action], and it cost her something, the [emotion]"
- "[body action] like [interpretive simile naming the state]"
- "She made [body part] [action], made [body part] [action]" 
  — the doubled "made" is the gloss-tell

PERMITTED:
- Body acts. Next sentence is a new image, new action, or 
  another body's response. The reader interprets.
- Simile permitted only if it INCREASES specificity of the 
  physical sensation, not if it names the emotional state. 
  "Like something thrown from a great distance by someone 
  with very good aim" = permitted (specifies the impact 
  vector, not the emotion). "Like a coin placed on a scale" 
  = banned (names the cost, glosses the state).

JOFFREY EXPRESSION FLOOR (and any analogous high-arrogance/
high-fixation young tyrant):

His obsession does not stay in his head. It comes out of his 
mouth, in front of witnesses, as:
- Boasts of what he will do to her when caught (specific, 
  sexual, violent, said in front of people who cannot leave)
- Demands for descriptions of her from anyone who has seen 
  her, followed by punishment of the describer for the 
  description being inadequate or too adequate
- Public contempt scaled to the private fixation (the louder 
  he says she is nothing, the more she is everything)
- Displacement onto present bodies: Sansa-substitutes, 
  serving girls, the bell-ringer, the Hound's dog, any 
  available target

He is NEVER alone with the obsession on the page. The 
obsession is a thing the court has to watch him have.

ANCHOR: Commodus in the historical record (Dio Cassius, 
Herodian) — public, performed, displaced onto gladiators 
and senators. Caligula on Drusilla. Nero on Poppaea after 
he killed her. The young-tyrant-obsession register is 
LOUD and WITNESSED, not interior and private.

SCENE EXIT AUDIT:

Before closing any scene at lattice ≥0.75: name the casualty.

A casualty is a named human present in the scene who exits 
in materially worse condition than they entered. "Materially 
worse" = struck, bleeding, demoted, dismissed, weeping, 
soiled themselves, ordered to die, watched a loved one 
ordered to die, lost something they cannot recover.

Meredith leaving with a fright is NOT a casualty. Meredith 
leaving with the imprint of fingers on her jaw and an order 
she knows will end with her sister in the black cells is a 
casualty.

No casualty named = scene did not reach amplitude. Restart.
